    "problem_statement": "# Auto-tick functionality in freezegun not working correctly\n\n## Description\n\nI've encountered an issue with the auto-tick functionality in freezegun. When using the auto-tick feature, the time doesn't increment as expected.\n\nGiven the following code:\n\n```python\nimport datetime\nimport freezegun\n\n# Freeze time with auto_tick_seconds\nwith freezegun.freeze_time(\"2020-01-01\", auto_tick_seconds=1.5) as frozen_time:\n    time1 = datetime.datetime.now()\n    time2 = datetime.datetime.now()\n    \n    # This should be true, but it's not\n    assert time2 > time1\n    assert (time2 - time1).total_seconds() == 1.5\n```\n\nThe assertion fails because the auto-tick functionality isn't working correctly. The second time value should be 1.5 seconds later than the first one, but they're either the same or the difference is incorrect.\n\nI've also tried combining auto-tick with manual ticking:\n\n```python\nwith freezegun.freeze_time(\"2020-01-01\", auto_tick_seconds=1.5) as frozen_time:\n    time1 = datetime.datetime.now()\n    frozen_time.tick()  # Should advance by 1.5 seconds\n    time2 = datetime.datetime.now()\n    \n    # This should be true, but it's not\n    assert (time2 - time1).total_seconds() == 1.5\n```\n\nBut this also doesn't work as expected. It seems like the `StepTickTimeFactory` class might have an issue with how it's handling the time increments.\n\nI'm happy to provide more details if needed!"
  },
